who would your party like to see  legislation on this issue well I welcome  the decision of the employment tribunal  I mean uber is amazing technology it's  fantastic what they've been able to do  but the concern is if technology like  that transfers the obligations that we  expect to put on any good business to  the rest of us to taxpayers to the rest  of society in terms of paternity leave  and annual holidays sick pay well that  isn't a reasonable position to have so  it's about the balance between the two  things and I think it's an excellent bit  of work from the GMB trade union nobody  wants to see anyone in work we denied  basic employment rights so it's a step  in the right direction technology should  free yourself itchin should liberate us  it shouldn't leave us in a position  where we enjoy any less employment  rights and mr. Reynolds said could I  just get your view on that other issue I  was discussing with mr. Hynes there the  issue of disability benefits and how  they're going to look at increasing  support and targeting they say do you  think they should go a lot further and  have a look at overall at the cuts  coming down the line absolutely I mean  first of all I welcome the government's  recognition that they're getting this  wrong this is a system which has not  helped people it is put a lot of people  into misery and quite a lot of suffering  the fundamental problem is it's punitive  you know when people get a letter  through the door saying they're getting  one of these assessments they don't  think brilliant i'm going to access some  more support they live in fear of that  assessment because it doesn't take into  account the medical situation it's an  administrative test it doesn't usually  give them in a position where they're  getting more supportive it leaves them  in a position where they're getting that  financial support taken away and the  fundamental problem has been the  government the Conservatives they've  tried to do welfare reform not in a way  which gets people back into work but in  a way which saves money and that's gonna  be the big question they've got to  answer when they take this consultation  forward now let's get onto your own  patch the city now are sitting in the  middle of that at the heart of the Bank  of England is of course the governor at  the moment Mark Carney do you think  there's an operation to try and  destabilizing by the government well it  seems completely reckless I mean when I  read the story this morning that some  code Conservative MPs wanted to replace  Mark Carney with Jacob rees-mogg after  say that was the scariest Halloween  story I could have read I mean this is  deeply irresponsible if you look at the  the fall in the pound befallen sterling  how will that be helped by forcing the  governor of the Bank of England out this  is deeply irresponsible Mark Carney did  the right thing after the brexit vote he  pacified at the economy with the  measures that were taken conservative  MPs can't blame the Bank of England for  the poor performance of the economy over  the last six years they've got to take  responsibility for that and this is not  helpful in any way to the brexit process  or to the state of the economy as we get  close to the Autumn Statement would you  sound a warning that if a government is  seen to of forced out or at least put  pressure on an independent governor of a  major central bank that might worry the  markets yeah absolutely I mean this in  no way would help anybody in Britain it  certainly wouldn't help our economy the  operational independence of the Bank of  England should be sacrosanct John  McDonald said exactly that in his speech  earlier in the week of course  politicians we can change the Mandate of  the inflation target for the Bank of  England we can change the Bank of  England act but once we've got those  things in place that operational  independence must be respected and the  lusting this country needs right now is  more this chaotic irresponsible behavior  from Conservative backbenchers now what  I ask you about the Richmond by-election  coming up next month we expect more if  we are in November which are more or  less imagining coming up a first of  December anyway do you genuinely believe  that Labour should not stand a candidate  in that by-election to maximize the  so-called progressive vote well that was  a personal view we are going to stand a  candidate I thought it was something  that was worth consideration if the  local party were agreeable to that but  that's not the direction we've taken I  respect that so i'll be out campaigning  for our labour candidate once they're  picked it we're going to play a full  role in that by-election because you've  had I mean you've returned to the front  bench haven't you didn't think much of  Jeremy Corbyn a few months ago now  you're back working with him why the  vault fast well it's brexit in the main  I think the brexit process is going to  be something completely fundamental to  my life to anyone who lives in Britain  now and in the political process for  many years to come and I'm deeply  worried I'm genuinely deeply worried by  what we've seen from the Conservative  Party so far not just this forcing out  the governor of the Bank of England  story but the messages we sent out on  foreign workers the failure to say EU  national should remain here we  to be now talking about a  sector-by-sector may be a factory by  factory brexit deal that's not the way  to get the best result for this country  in that process this negotiation has to  be scrutinized in parliament and of  course I want to play a part and I think  that's what any MP needs to be doing  right now and just tell me as shadow  City Minister and what kind of soundings  are you taking in the city the mum you  having a lot of lunches and a good  reception can you tell me who you've  been to see yes I've had quite a good  time in the city so far obviously the  sea isn't just a 